kraft - a coding agent, written in C, for FreeBSD first.

You describe a task; the model reads and changes files and runs
commands to do it, asking before anything that changes the system.
It speaks the Anthropic Messages API and OpenAI-compatible Chat
Completions APIs, so it works with Claude and with local servers
such as LM Studio, llama.cpp, vLLM and Ollama.

Building
--------

  make && make test
  make install            # PREFIX=/usr/local by default

or from the ports overlay: devel/kraft.  Needs only the base system
(libedit and OpenSSL); pdftotext (graphics/poppler-utils) is optional,
for reading PDFs with servers that cannot take them.

Claude, through the Anthropic API
---------------------------------

Put your API key in ~/.kraft/anthropic.key, readable only by you (or
set ANTHROPIC_API_KEY):

  mkdir -p ~/.kraft && chmod 700 ~/.kraft
  printf '%s\n' 'sk-ant-...' > ~/.kraft/anthropic.key
  chmod 600 ~/.kraft/anthropic.key

Then, in a project:

  kraft -u https://api.anthropic.com -m claude-opus-5
  kraft -u https://api.anthropic.com -m claude-opus-5 'make fails; fix it'
  kraft -u https://api.anthropic.com -m claude-opus-5 -w   # with web search

With Claude, requests use prompt caching, PDFs are read as documents,
and -w lets the model search the web.

A local model with LM Studio (or anything OpenAI-compatible)
------------------------------------------------------------

LM Studio serves both API formats; kraft speaks the Anthropic one by
default:

  kraft -u http://192.168.1.10:1234 -m qwen/qwen3.8-27b
  kraft -a openai -u http://192.168.1.10:1234/v1 -m qwen/qwen3.8-27b

For llama.cpp, vLLM, Ollama and other OpenAI-compatible servers, use
-a openai with the server's base URL.  If the server's API key is
needed, put it in ~/.kraft/openai.key or OPENAI_API_KEY.

Settings
--------

Rather than flags every time, ~/.kraft/config:

  url https://api.anthropic.com
  model claude-opus-5
  mode auto
  allow bash make test*
  allow edit src/*
  deny bash git push*

Then plain `kraft` will do.  A project may have a .kraft/config of
its own; kraft asks before using one, and again if it changes.

Permission modes (-P, or "mode" in the config):

  ask     ask before every edit and command (the default)
  rules   allow what fixed safety rules find harmless; ask about the rest
  auto    the rules, then a check by the model for what they cannot settle
  yes     never ask (only where the model can do no harm)

At an approval prompt: y yes, n no, a always this session, p always
here (saved as a rule in .kraft/config).  ^C stops the reply or
command in progress.

MCP servers (commands, or URLs for remote ones) add their tools; every call
is asked about unless a rule allows it:

  mcp fs npx -y @modelcontextprotocol/server-filesystem /home/me/notes
  allow mcp__fs__* *
  mcp gh https://api.githubcopilot.com/mcp/
  mcp_header gh Authorization: Bearer ${GITHUB_TOKEN}

Hooks run your commands around tool calls and after tasks:

  hook after edit case "$KRAFT_PATH" in *.c) clang-format -i "$KRAFT_PATH";; esac
  hook done printf '\a'

With devel/universal-ctags installed the model also gets a symbols
tool, to find definitions without reading whole files.

At the prompt
-------------

  @file.png       attach an image (or, with Claude, a PDF) to a task
  ... \           end a line with a backslash to go on to the next
  /undo           put back the files the last task changed
  /diff /commit   see the uncommitted changes, or have them committed
  /compact        summarize the conversation to save context
  /usage          tokens, cache hits, context use
  /rules /mode    the permission rules and hooks, and the mode
  /mcp            the MCP servers and their tools
  /help           all of them

kraft -r resumes the last session in the current directory.

See kraft(1) for everything, and HACKING for how the code is laid out.
